\section*{Introduction}

Sentiment analysis plays a critical role in natural language processing, with applications ranging from customer feedback assessment to social media sentiment tracking. While sentiment classifiers have been extensively studied, their behavior in the presence of linguistic phenomena such as negation remains underexplored. Negation words, including 'not', 'never', and 'no', are known to invert the sentiment of a sentence, yet their influence on model performance, particularly in short texts, is not well understood. This study investigates whether sentiment classifiers are sensitive to the presence or absence of negation words in short movie reviews from the SST-2 dataset. 

The SST-2 dataset, a commonly used benchmark for sentiment classification, contains short movie reviews annotated as either positive or negative. By systematically modifying these reviews through the addition or removal of negation words, this research seeks to evaluate how sentiment classifiers respond to such linguistic variations. The findings of this study can offer insights into the robustness of sentiment analysis models and contribute to the development of more accurate and context-aware sentiment classification systems. Furthermore, the results are expected to enhance the understanding of how linguistic features affect model performance and provide guidance for improving the reliability of sentiment analysis in practical applications.

